{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y56FwAfxHk7WVjMPmfZcKF","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tid: rel03.1-uc002-arch\n2\ttitle: \"Print machine hardware name via arch\"\n3\t\n4\tsummary: |\n5\t  The operator invokes the Go arch binary to print the machine hardware name (e.g.,\n6\t  x86_64, arm64), equivalent to uname -m. The differential testing harness runs both\n7\t  the Go binary and the Homebrew reference binary (garch from coreutils) with the same\n8\t  arguments and compares stdout byte-for-byte.\n9\t\n10\tactor: Human Operator\n11\t\n12\ttrigger: |\n13\t  The operator has completed the PRD (srd045-arch.yaml) and invokes do-work to\n14\t  produce a passing differential test for cmd/arch.\n15\t\n16\tflow:\n17\t  - id: F1\n18\t    step: |\n19\t      Build cmd/arch via mage build. Requires no prior steps.\n20\t  - id: F2\n21\t    step: |\n22\t      The differential testing harness (pkg/testutils) executes both ./bin/arch and\n23\t      garch with identical arguments and environment, comparing stdout byte-for-byte\n24\t      and exit codes. Requires F1 (binary must be built).\n25\t\n26\ttouchpoints:\n27\t  - T1: \"cmd/arch — machine hardware name output (srd045-arch R1, R2)\"\n28\t  - T2: \"pkg/testutils — RunDiffTests, stdout comparison\"\n29\t  - T3: \"pkg/sys — InstallSIGPIPEHandler\"\n30\t\n31\tsuccess_criteria:\n32\t  - id: S1\n33\t    criterion: |\n34\t      The differential test passes for normal invocation and error cases (extra\n35\t      operand, unknown flag), confirming full parity with garch.\n36\t    traces:\n37\t      - AC1\n38\t      - AC2\n39\t      - AC3\n40\t\n41\tout_of_scope:\n42\t  - Processor type (-p) and hardware platform (-i) are not reported by arch;\n43\t    use uname for those fields.\n44\t\n45\ttest_suite: test-rel03.1\n46\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01FPZ28N3Fbimesc67hSRaTY","session_id":"acec8f0f-580e-45e5-9e80-e371a86b1d82","uuid":"3839fa52-38dc-4380-a1b7-06ee40578d12","timestamp":"2026-05-14T16:53:22.019Z"}
